WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.060
There are more and more
electric-powered ferries in the world,

00:00:03.060 --> 00:00:05.580
at least for short distances,
across rivers or small lakes.

00:00:05.580 --> 00:00:07.080
But one of the big problems with them

00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:10.440
is that the batteries required
are massive and expensive.

00:00:10.440 --> 00:00:12.900
A ferry not only has to move something
the size of a building,

00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:15.180
but it's got to push the water
out of the way too,

00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:18.480
so you need far more energy
than something like an electric car.

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:21.240
Electric ferries would be
a lot easier to run

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:23.640
if you could just, y'know,
plug them into the mains.

00:00:23.640 --> 00:00:27.420
So that's what the
Randers Fjords Ferry in Denmark does:

00:00:27.420 --> 00:00:31.560
it has 350 metres of
hardened, waterproof, electrical cable

00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:32.880
rolled up on a drum.

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:34.500
One end is connected to the ferry.

00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:36.960
The other end is plugged into the
electrical grid, just there,

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:39.485
and as we set off,
all the power for the motors

00:00:39.485 --> 00:00:40.865
will be coming from the shore.

00:00:40.865 --> 00:00:42.105
IN DANISH:

00:01:14.304 --> 00:01:17.940
The most surprising thing for me
is just how quiet this ferry is.

00:01:17.940 --> 00:01:19.800
There's no diesel motor
churning away.

00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:23.520
And you might be thinking, okay,
what if something snags on the cable?

00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:26.040
Well, this was already
a cable-guided ferry,

00:01:26.040 --> 00:01:26.940
even before it was electric.

00:01:26.940 --> 00:01:29.700
Cable ferries are pretty common
around the world in places like this,

00:01:29.700 --> 00:01:31.980
channels with strong currents
or shallow waters.

00:01:31.980 --> 00:01:35.700
Instead of being pushed by a propeller,
the boat pulls itself across

00:01:35.700 --> 00:01:38.100
by latching onto two cables
laid across the riverbed.

00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:39.960
There's not much boat traffic round here,

00:01:39.960 --> 00:01:43.260
and there are already clear rules for
how to navigate around cables like that:

00:01:43.260 --> 00:01:46.471
basically, don't pass while the ferry's in motion.

00:02:15.382 --> 00:02:16.651
We're not going to be seeing this on

00:02:16.651 --> 00:02:18.829
ferries across the English Channel
any time soon.

00:02:18.829 --> 00:02:22.320
Plug-in boats are obviously going
to be limited to a few situations.

00:02:22.320 --> 00:02:27.180
And the biggest fuel-burners are still
massive world-travelling container ships.

00:02:27.180 --> 00:02:31.620
But this does feel like
one more step in the right direction.

